  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nZonal flow in the Pacific Northwest today will evolve into a \nshortwave trough across the Northern Plains tomorrow, with the\nridging over the Four Corners region strengthening and expanding\neastward to fill in behind the wave. Cloud cover may still\nlinger over southern portions of the area on Saturday, which\nwould keep temperatures a few degrees cooler, but temperatures\ngenerally are expected to be a seasonally appropriate mid- to \nupper 70s. Under mostly clear skies on Sunday, temperatures\ncould nose as high as low 80s, before a dry cold front moves\nsouth through the area late Sunday into Monday in association \nwith the wave in the Northern Plains.\n\nTemperatures briefly drop a few degrees back into the 70s in the\nwake of the cold front on Monday, but they don't stay there\nlong. By Tuesday, ridging builds back across the area, with a\ngradual warming trend in the deterministic NBM guidance keeping\nus in the 80s most of next week, warming to upper 80s again by\nnext Friday. NBM 25th-75th percentile temperature spreads are \nonly 2-4 degrees for most of the area through Friday, indicating\nthat models are well in agreement that this outcome will occur.\nTemperature spreads are slightly larger further north into\ncentral Missouri (5-6 degrees) which would align with LREF\ncluster analysis that suggests a late-week frontal boundary \ncould sag south towards the region. NBM PoPs for Friday are an\nastounding 1% for about half of our CWA, which is a real bright\nspot in an otherwise warm and completely dry forecast.",
